1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
The prompting playbook in four moves, evals first control edge and boundary cases so every

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,960
change is measured, not guessed.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,960
Hygiene second, XML structure, clear role, output contract, if you cannot tell policy

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,160
from guidelines, neither can the model.

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:17,400
Patch debt third, old defensive instructions break on new models, version control the why,

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,880
not just the what.

7
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,120
Capability gap fourth, instructions do not add capability.

8
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:26,440
If a task needs reliable arithmetic or a handoff, give the model a tool or a human

9
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:28,000
escalation path.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,840
A prompt that works is debugged like code, reproducible cases, one failure mode at a time.

11
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:37,640
Anthropic engineer, Margaret Vanler ran the real prompting playbook on a real production

12
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,480
prompt and this is Liam in for bear.

13
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:44,240
Two scenarios, a prompt that worked fine until the model migration and a new agentic

14
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:45,920
system built from zero.

15
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,560
One discipline applies to both evals first, hygiene second, failure modes one at a time,

16
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:54,440
tools where instructions fall short, let's actually look at it.

17
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,840
Engineers face two prompting scenarios.

18
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,680
Scenario one, a prompt in production, multiple contributors, no clear owner, patches

19
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:05,400
layered for previous models and a migration suddenly breaks half the test cases.

20
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,480
Scenario two, a new agent from scratch, no baseline picking a model, a harness and a first

21
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:11,720
draft with nothing to hill climb against.

22
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,640
The same discipline applies to both.

23
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,920
You need a reproducible test suite before you touch anything.

24
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:21,080
Before targeting failure modes, you need an eval suite, not optional mandatory.

25
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,920
Three categories, control cases, unambiguous should always pass confirms the model

26
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,640
handles the basics.

27
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,200
Change cases, failures you've seen before, the prompt now catches them explicitly so they

28
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,760
can't silently regress.

29
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:35,960
Boundary cases, when to escalate to a human or refuse outright, the model needs a clear sense

30
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:39,120
of where its role ends, without all three columns.

31
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:44,400
You cannot tell if a prompt change was an improvement or just a different kind of wrong.

32
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:47,080
General hygiene before targeting specific failures.

33
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:51,080
Rule of thumb, if you cannot tell guidelines from policy from data when reading the prompt,

34
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:53,600
the model cannot either, the fix is structure.

35
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,880
XML tags, separating role, guidelines, policy, tone, customer context and the user message

36
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,200
that clean up alone improved performance on a failing scenario before any targeted instruction

37
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:09,320
change, separately an output contract, a stop sequence on the closing XML tag tells the

38
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:12,120
model exactly when to stop.

39
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:16,960
Disciplined output structure matters most with nested JSON or structured schemas.

40
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,440
Here's a failure mode you won't find in a textbook.

41
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:23,960
The customer's data includes exactly how much hotspot data they have, five gigabytes.

42
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:27,360
The model tells them to check the account URL instead of answering directly.

43
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:28,360
Why?

44
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,160
An old instruction never give a customer wrong plan details point them to the URL.

45
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,360
That instruction was written to stop hallucination on a previous model.

46
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:37,880
Newer models follow instructions more literally.

47
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:39,800
The patch became the bug.

48
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:44,680
Best practice, version control your defensive instructions, track why they exist.

49
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:52,520
An instruction written to stop a 2023 hallucination may cause 2025 information withholding.

50
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:54,360
Instructions do not add capability.

51
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,920
Critical always calculate proration amounts correctly.

52
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:58,480
That instruction does nothing.

53
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:02,560
The model cannot reliably do mental arithmetic telling it to do mental arithmetic correctly

54
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:03,560
is not an implementation.

55
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:04,560
It is a prayer.

56
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:09,000
The fix was a tool whenever a calculation is needed, call the proration calculator.

57
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,160
The math runs in deterministic code.

58
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:14,400
The model reads the result and explains it to the customer.

59
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:19,720
If you need reliable precision on a task, arithmetic, date, logic, database, lookup, define the

60
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:24,560
tool, register it in the API, let the model decide when to call it.

61
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:27,400
The billing error case, a customer has a billing conflict.

62
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:29,920
The model tries to diagnose it instead of escalating.

63
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:30,920
Why?

64
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:35,120
The prompt said avoid escalating unless absolutely necessary escalations cost approximately

65
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:39,560
$8 per case and count against fast resolution metrics, one side of the trade off.

66
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,720
No mention of the cost of getting it wrong, a refund plus customer trust models become

67
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:47,400
more capable means they optimize trade offs more aggressively.

68
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:52,640
If you give them an incomplete cost function, they solve the wrong problem, state both sides,

69
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:57,320
the cost of escalating and the cost of not escalating, let the model weigh them.

70
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:02,640
New agent from scratch, a retail staff scheduler with hard constraints, four approaches tested.

71
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,240
Sonnet 4.6 with a simple prompt, all five trials fail, Opus 4.7 same prompt violations

72
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:09,240
drop significantly still failing.

73
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:12,960
Opus with extended thinking, compliance schedules, but triple the tokens and triple

74
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:13,960
the latency.

75
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:19,640
Sonnet 4.6 with a better prompt to a five pass, but the model hits output limits before finishing.

76
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:24,880
Model prompt and harness are three separate levers pulling any one of them in isolation often

77
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,200
isn't enough.

78
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:30,360
The winning configuration for the scheduler, three simple prompts instead of one complex

79
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,360
one.

80
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,240
Generator creates a draft schedule.

81
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:37,440
Evaluator checks every hard constraint report specific violations with evidence repair

82
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:42,080
prompt receives those violations and makes targeted fixes fewer tokens lower latency

83
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:46,800
than sonnet with an enlarged prompt bonus soft constraints at runtime Harry doesn't like

84
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:50,000
working with Sally goes into the evaluator description.

85
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,160
No code changes to the validation function required.

86
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:57,880
The pattern works because each prompt has one job when a complex task has clear separable

87
00:04:57,880 --> 00:04:59,880
steps separate them.

